Search for "w" in the codebase might be too generic. Let's search for "kinematic points" perhaps.

But maybe easier: Look at the tool definition for exclurad_generate_input to see required structure: We'll need to call it with "points" as an array of objects containing likely fields: "w", "q2", "cos_theta", "phi". Also maybe "label"? Actually "label" is an optional argument for label of input file.

Let's examine tool spec in detail:

```
type exclurad_generate_input = (_: {
// Points
//
// Write EXCLURAD input file(s) for the given kinematic points,
// byte-compatible with the validated input format (10-point chunking, blank
// lines, trailer line). Runs preflight first and refuses on FAIL unless
// skip_preflight is set; a manifest.csv accompanies the files.
//
// beam_gev defaults to the campaign the channel's reference data came from,
// not the only valid energy — the reply echoes which one was used and lists
// the alternatives. rc_mode is "full" (the validated O(alpha) calculation)
// or "leading_log" (a faster APPROXIMATION); 0/1 are accepted too.
points: // Probable Type
[
  {
    },
],
// Outdir
//
// Output directory to write input files to (must already exist)
// outdir: string,
// Beam Gev
//
// beam_gev defaults to the campaign the channel's reference data came from,
// not the only valid energy — the reply echoes which one was used and lists
// the alternatives. rc_mode (the input file's second field) is 0 = full O(alpha)
// or 1 = factorizable leading-log; the approximation runs ~50x faster but differs
// by ~7% in delta and returns zero radiative correction to the beam-spin asymmetry
// — every validated result used mode 0.
beam_gev?: number | null,
// Vcut
//
// vcut is the maximum invariant mass of the final-state hadronic system (W) that
// is allowed; points with larger W are rejected as unphysical. Defaults to the
// channel's default_vcut_gev2? Actually from earlier: "vcut" is the maximum invariant
// mass of the final-state hadronic system (W) that is allowed; points with larger W
// are rejected as unphysical. Defaults to the channel's default vcut value.
vcut?: number | null,
// Rc Mode
//
// RC_MODE is an input file field; either 0 or 1 are accepted: 0 runs the full O(alpha)
// calculation; 1 uses a speed‑up approximation. The full calculation may hang or fail
// (run with a timer!) for extreme unphysical kinematics, especially near threshold.
// It defaults to 0.
rc_mode?: number | string,
// Label
//
// A human‑readable label used to name the generated input files. If not given,
// defaults to "rcgrid".  This is also used for the manifest.csv filename.
label?: string,
// Skip Preflight
//
// Normally EXCLURAD aborts on unphysical kinematics; set this to true to suppress
// that safety check. Only use this for quick tests.
skip_preflight?: boolean,
// Work Dir
//
// Used internally to locate and read lookup tables for the channel; you should not
// need to set this unless you know what you’re doing.
work_dir?: string | null,
}) => any;
```

Thus to call generate_input, we need to provide channel name, points array with objects. Each point likely must include fields w, q2, cos_theta, phi. But maybe also "costheta"? Let's check other tool usage patterns: In exclurad_preflight_check spec: points are dict: {"w": GeV, "q2": GeV^2, "cos_theta*: [-1,1], "phi": deg}. So fields: "w", "q2", "cos_theta*", "phi". Possibly the key "cos_theta*" includes asterisk; but that seems odd as a JSON key (with *). Maybe it's just "cos_theta". Or maybe it's "cos_theta" but description shows "*" as footnote.

Let's search for "cos_theta*" exactly in codebase.

